course: dictionary
lesson: 12190
title: A formal word: conferral
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Conferral is a formal word for officially giving someone something important. Read this: «The conferral of the award took place on Friday, in front of the whole school.» The clues are official, in front of the whole school, and award. So conferral means the official giving of an award.
explain: Look at the ending first. The ending -al often turns a verb into a noun. The verb here is confer, so conferral is the act of conferring. Try swapping in "giving": «The giving of the award took place on Friday.» It still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Notice the feeling of the word. In everyday talk people say «giving» or «being given». Writers choose conferral when they want to show that the gift was official and came from someone with authority, such as a university, a head teacher or the government.
explain: Conferral has a rare second meaning: talking things over with others. You will usually see that sense written as «conferring» or «conference». In texts about awards, degrees and prizes, the first meaning is almost always the one you need.
fact: Conferral means the act of giving someone an honour, a title, a degree or a right in an official way.
fact: Conferral is a noun, and it is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in official writing.
fact: The verb it comes from is «confer», so conferral is the act of conferring.
fact: In everyday speech, people say «giving» or «being given» instead of conferral.
fact: The rare second meaning, talking things over with others, is usually written as «conferring» or «conference».
exercise: «The conferral of the award took place on Friday.» What does conferral mean here? | A test with many questions | A long walk to the school gate | A loud argument in the hall | *The official giving of an award
exercise: Which everyday word could replace conferral in «the conferral of an award»? | hiding | sleeping | *giving | breaking
exercise: Which verb is conferral made from? | conceal | confetti | *confer | confide
exercise: «The school made the conferral of a prize to Amara for her kindness.» What is the best meaning of conferral? | *The official giving of a prize | A game played in the playground | A rule for lunchtime | The loud sound of a bell
test: Which sentence uses conferral correctly? | I had a conferral of cheese sandwiches for lunch. | *The university announced the conferral of degrees in July. | The conferral ran across the muddy field. | My dog did a conferral on the sofa.
test: In «The conferral of citizenship was held at the town hall», what does conferral mean? | *The official giving of a right, here citizenship | A type of sandwich | A cosy chat between two friends | A quiet walk in the park
exam: In «The head teacher's conferral of the prize was met with cheers», which everyday phrase says the same thing? | was lost on the way to the hall | was wrapped in paper | *was given the prize by the head teacher | was argued about at lunch
